{#
    A frame, and — where it is a specimen — the caption that says what it is.

    Two nodes share this template because they are the same thing: a document
    of somebody else's, shown inside this one. A video has a title and nothing
    else to say; a specimen names the card and the size it was measured at,
    because that number is checked by `make fit` and a reader who wants to know
    what they are looking at should not have to open the source.

    **The element, and nothing but the address of it.** What the two need is a
    frame that is sized, bordered and captioned the way every other block on
    the page is, and `sds-embed` is where that is decided once — see its own
    file for the two shapes and why an embed has to state which one it is. Left
    to the renderer this was a raw frame with the browser's 1996 ridge around
    it, as wide as its `width` attribute said and running straight out of any
    column narrower than that.

    The `<iframe>` used to be written here and lifted by the element. It is a
    property now, like everything else: `make guides` renders each element
    before the page is published — `scripts/lib/prerender.ts` — so a reader
    with no JavaScript has the frame without this file having to draw one.

    Not lazy, and that is the element's decision rather than this file's: a
    specimen is the evidence on the page, and a frame that loads on scroll is
    a frame that is blank in every screenshot the gate takes — which is the one
    place somebody looks at all of them at once.
#}
{%- if node.hasOption('specimen') -%}
{#- Fixed. The card declares the viewport it was measured at and this is that
    number, so the frame keeps it and scrolls where the column is narrower;
    a card reflowed to fit would be documenting a layout nothing checked.

    The caption is a sentence and arrives as one. It used to carry the viewport
    in `.sds-mono`, which is a register this file was choosing on the page's
    behalf — what a caption says belongs to the document, how it is set belongs
    to the component. -#}
<sds-embed src="{{ asset(node.url) }}"
    label="{{ node.option('title')|default('Specimen') }}"
    caption="{{ node.option('title')|default('Specimen') }} · {{ node.option('viewport') }}"
    {%- if node.hasOption('width') %} width="{{ node.option('width') }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if node.hasOption('height') %} height="{{ node.option('height') }}"{% endif -%}
></sds-embed>
{%- else -%}
{#- Fluid. A player's `width` and `height` are its native size and not the
    size it wants: it fills the column it is given and holds the shape those
    two state, which is the one thing about it that must not change. -#}
<sds-embed src="{{ node.url }}"
    ratio="{{ node.option('width')|default(560) }} / {{ node.option('height')|default(315) }}"
    {%- if node.hasOption('width') %} width="{{ node.option('width') }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if node.hasOption('height') %} height="{{ node.option('height') }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if node.hasOption('title') %} label="{{ node.option('title') }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if node.hasOption('allow') %} allow="{{ node.option('allow') }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if node.hasOption('allowfullscreen') and node.option('allowfullscreen') %} allowfullscreen{% endif -%}
></sds-embed>
{%- endif -%}
